- [acc-intentions-044] Deploy AI Advisory Systems for High-Stakes Government Decision-Making: Optimism Bias — The node presupposes that AI can provide 'calm, analytical, and unbiased advice' in value-laden crises, overstating current capabilities and understating the brittleness of AI systems outside their training distribution.
- [acc-desires-026] Deploying AI to Run Society at Scale: Nirvana Fallacy — Advocates implicitly compare AI governance to an idealized optimum rather than to realistic, imperfect human alternatives, ignoring that AI systems bring their own failure modes, biases, and unintended consequences at scale.
- [acc-beliefs-069] Output Evidence Suffices Only for Metric-Legible Harm Classes: Streetlight Effect — Privileging outputs simply because they are measurable, while ignoring internal states that are harder to assess but potentially more diagnostically important for safety.
- [acc-beliefs-069] Output Evidence Suffices Only for Metric-Legible Harm Classes: False Dichotomy — The claim frames output-based and internals-based understanding as mutually exclusive, when in practice the most robust AI safety approaches combine both behavioral evaluation and mechanistic interpretability.
- [acc-desires-009] Automate Society's Operating System: nirvana fallacy — The vision implicitly benchmarks messy, imperfect human governance against an idealized AI operating system, ignoring the equally imperfect and potentially far more dangerous failure modes of the proposed alternative.
- [acc-intentions-054] Ground AI Safety in Observable Behavior and Real-World Testing: Hasty Generalization — The framework assumes that behaviors observed in real-world testing will generalize to the full distribution of deployment scenarios, but AI systems can behave safely across a wide test set and still fail catastrophically in underrepresented edge cases.
- [acc-intentions-047] Argue That AI Requires Entirely New Laws, Not Adapted Old Ones: False Dilemma — The argument frames the choice as binary — entirely new frameworks versus unchanged old ones — ignoring that existing legal doctrines (tort, contract, administrative law) can be substantially reinterpreted or supplemented without wholesale replacement.
- [acc-intentions-047] Argue That AI Requires Entirely New Laws, Not Adapted Old Ones: Novelty (Appeal to Novelty) — The core premise assumes that because AI is new, it categorically requires new law, conflating technological novelty with legal incompatibility — a non-sequitur, as transformative technologies like electricity and the internet were largely governed under adapted existing frameworks.

